---
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";

export const frontmatter = {
  title: "How to develop custom visuals using Figma and AI",
  description: "How I use Figma and AI to build custom data visualizations by using SVG as a technical anchor.",
  pubDate: "2026-05-09",
  tags: ["Data Visualization", "AI", "Figma", "D3.js", "Looker Studio"],
};

const currentLang = "en";
const alternateHref = "/es/blog/custom-visuals-workflow-figma-ai/";

const lipstickVideo = "/assets/imgs/Projects/lookerstudio_lipstick_viz_optimized.mp4";
---

<MainLayout
  title="How to develop custom visuals using Figma and AI | Alexis Gomel"
  description="How I use Figma and AI to build custom data visualizations by using SVG as a technical anchor."
  currentLang={currentLang}
  alternateHref={alternateHref}
>
  <style is:inline>
    .blog-wrap {
      max-width: 760px;
      margin: 0 auto;
      padding: 120px 24px 80px;
      line-height: 1.8;
      font-size: 1.05rem;
      color: #333;
    }
    .blog-wrap h1 {
      font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 4vw, 2.2rem);
      font-weight: 700;
      line-height: 1.25;
      margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    }
    .blog-meta {
      font-size: 0.85rem;
      color: #888;
      margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
    }
    .blog-wrap h2 {
      font-size: 1.35rem;
      font-weight: 700;
      margin-top: 2.5rem;
      margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
      border-bottom: 2px solid #f0f0f0;
      padding-bottom: 0.3rem;
    }
    .blog-wrap p {
      margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
    }
    .blog-video {
      width: 100%;
      border-radius: 8px;
      border: 1px solid #e5e5e5;
      box-shadow: 0 4px 16px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.07);
      margin: 1rem 0 0.4rem;
    }
    .img-caption {
      font-size: 0.8rem;
      color: #999;
      text-align: center;
      margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    }
    .tag {
      display: inline-block;
      background: #f4f4f4;
      border-radius: 4px;
      padding: 2px 10px;
      font-size: 0.78rem;
      color: #555;
      margin-right: 6px;
      margin-bottom: 1rem;
    }
    .highlight-box {
      background: #f9f9f9;
      padding: 1.5rem;
      border-radius: 8px;
      border: 1px solid #eee;
      margin: 2rem 0;
      font-family: monospace;
      font-size: 0.9rem;
      overflow-x: auto;
    }
    .cta-box {
      background: #f8f8f8;
      border-left: 4px solid var(--primary, #3498db);
      padding: 24px;
      border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;
      margin: 2.5rem 0;
      text-align: center;
    }
    .cta-button {
      display: inline-block;
      background: #3498db;
      color: white;
      padding: 12px 24px;
      border-radius: 6px;
      text-decoration: none;
      font-weight: 600;
      margin-top: 1rem;
      transition: background 0.2s;
    }
    .cta-button:hover {
      background: #2980b9;
      color: white;
    }
  </style>

  <article class="blog-wrap">
    <span class="tag">Data Visualization</span>
    <span class="tag">AI</span>
    <span class="tag">Figma</span>
    <span class="tag">D3.js</span>

    <h1>How to develop custom visuals using Figma and AI</h1>
    
    <p class="blog-meta">
      Alexis Gomel · 9 May 2026
    </p>

    <video autoplay muted loop playsinline class="blog-video">
      <source src={lipstickVideo} type="video/mp4" />
      Your browser does not support the video tag.
    </video>
    <p class="img-caption">The Lipstick viz. I built this to compare real values against targets without using a cluttered table.</p>

    <p>
      I am not a professional developer. I can read D3.js syntax, but writing it from scratch takes too much effort. It feels disconnected from my workflow. I think in stories. I like Figma because it keeps the focus on the user experience.
    </p>

    <p>
      I found a technical anchor for this. I use SVG as a shared language between my design and the AI agent. This makes prototyping fast.
    </p>

    <h2>The friction of natural language</h2>
    <p>
      Natural language is a terrible way to describe a complex chart. You spend hours in loops trying to get the agent to see what you mean. Figma is different. It is great for design, but it produces dead code.
    </p>
    <p>
      The SVG export solves this. When I export from Figma, I am giving the AI a blueprint. It contains the geometry and the CSS classes needed for the final code. Even with modern AI vision, having the SVG code provides a grounding that prevents the model from hallucinating the layout. I find that naming my layers intentionally in Figma makes this anchor even stronger.
    </p>

    <h2>Why the Lipstick visual?</h2>
    <p>
      I chose the Lipstick visual for my first experiment because it solved a layout problem. Usually, comparing actual metrics against targets requires two separate things. You need a bar plot for the visual and a table for the numbers. It takes up too much space.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Lipstick visual packs all that data into a single shape. It includes the real value alongside the target. It removes the need for a table.
    </p>

    <h2>Figma as a storyboard</h2>
    <p>
      My workflow is a mix of spec-driven and iterative design. I use Figma like a movie storyboard. It is where I fail fast with layouts. I don't want to test concepts with code yet.
    </p>
    <p>
      Once the core idea is solid, I move to a local HTML environment. This is where I fail fast on bugs and animations. The local setup uses a mock of the target platform.
    </p>

    <div class="highlight-box">
<pre>
const testData = &#123;
  tables: &#123;
    DEFAULT: [
      &#123; dimensionCol: ["Region A"], valueCol: [80], targetCol: [100] &#125;,
      &#123; dimensionCol: ["Region B"], valueCol: [120], targetCol: [100] &#125;
    ]
  &#125;
&#125;;
</pre>
    </div>

    <p>
      I ask the agent for this data immediately. We plot it and see the visual live. We tweak the hovers and transitions in a simplified setting. This cycle is fast because the foundation is there. I focus on the aesthetics while the agent handles the math.
    </p>

    <p>
      Speed of failure is a core feature of this workflow. By testing in a controlled HTML environment before moving to Looker Studio, I can run multiple iterations in the time it would take to refresh a live dashboard just once.
    </p>

    <div class="cta-box">
      <p style="font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Check out the finished visuals</p>
      <p>I've used this method to build a gallery of custom components, from the Lipstick chart to interactive Tree Hierarchies.</p>
      <a href="/projects/looker-studio-custom-visuals/" class="cta-button">
        Explore Custom Visuals &rarr;
      </a>
    </div>

    <p>
      This approach feels natural. It uses the multimodal power of modern AI to connect the design UI directly to production code. I don't need to be a D3 expert to build something unique.
    </p>
  </article>
</MainLayout>
